WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.250
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:06.000
Uh, and today I wanna talk about
why I learn languages and I want to

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.750
use as an example, Persian, which
is what I'm learning right now.

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:12.690
If you enjoy these videos,
please subscribe, click on

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:14.070
the bell for notifications.

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:17.370
If you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a review.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.640
So, uh, if any of you read my book,
The Linguist, A Personal Guide to

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:29.849
Language Learning uh, you will remember
that I always get interested in the

00:00:29.849 --> 00:00:34.605
history, the culture, countries that
influence other countries, how countries

00:00:34.605 --> 00:00:39.075
are an amalgamation of different
influences and, and, and peoples.

00:00:39.075 --> 00:00:40.515
And that's been the history of the world.

00:00:40.725 --> 00:00:44.715
And that's what kind of motivates me
to learn languages and learn about all

00:00:44.715 --> 00:00:46.125
these different cultures and so forth.

00:00:46.425 --> 00:00:47.745
Persian is no exception.

00:00:47.835 --> 00:00:52.785
I'm very lucky that I have a, a Persian
tutor, collaborator in Iran who has

00:00:52.785 --> 00:00:57.605
created tremendous content for me on
the history of Iran, foods of Iran, has

00:00:57.605 --> 00:00:59.795
interviews with ordinary people in Iran.

00:00:59.945 --> 00:01:05.285
So I have a bit of a smattering of
that background, but as with, when

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:08.795
we learn languages and we learn and
forget words, things that I learn

00:01:08.795 --> 00:01:12.605
about, the history of a country, I
tend to forget them as well, but every

00:01:12.605 --> 00:01:15.065
time I forget them, something remains.

00:01:15.065 --> 00:01:20.295
And so my appreciation of the
history does improve a little bit

00:01:20.325 --> 00:01:25.455
each time, but it gives me a basis
from which to learn more things.

00:01:25.604 --> 00:01:32.414
And so right now, uh, at LingQ, I am
doing a series of four episodes of

00:01:32.414 --> 00:01:38.100
a podcast about Iranian identity put
out by this podcast service called

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:41.700
epitome.ir or epitome books.ir.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:46.080
I'll leave a link to the, to, to the,
uh, podcast in the description box.

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:49.500
It's all in, uh, in Farsi,
in Persian, of course.

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:56.054
And so I am just enjoying so much
participating in this series of

00:01:56.054 --> 00:02:02.145
discussions, uh, and the narrator quotes
different books where the question

00:02:02.145 --> 00:02:04.215
of Iranian identity is discussed.

00:02:04.605 --> 00:02:09.854
So I have the sort of sensation that
I am participating in a discussion

00:02:10.305 --> 00:02:14.804
amongst Iranians about their
identity and through that, I am

00:02:14.804 --> 00:02:16.725
exposed to aspects of their history.

00:02:16.845 --> 00:02:20.625
Some of which is new to me, some of
which I have already been exposed to.

00:02:20.804 --> 00:02:24.015
And so the whole experience
to me is, is my reward.

00:02:24.075 --> 00:02:28.035
That's why I learn languages, to
be able to do that kind of thing.

00:02:28.155 --> 00:02:30.269
And I've done it in Ukrainian.

00:02:30.269 --> 00:02:34.350
When my, uh, Ukrainian tutor sent
me material on Ukrainian history,

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:37.680
I've done it in Polish when I went
to this website and found audiobooks

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:39.269
and eBooks about Polish history.

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:41.220
These are the things
that I'm interested in.

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:45.690
Czeck the, uh, ... about Czeck history.

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:45.959
Those...

00:02:45.959 --> 00:02:49.740
that's what drives me to learn
languages, but let's get back to Persian.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:54.180
And I also wanted to comment on Persian
because someone asked me here, uh,

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:58.130
on my YouTube channel, what has been
my reaction to learning pPersian.

00:02:58.395 --> 00:03:03.345
Well, first of all, there are a lot
of Iranian immigrants in Vancouver

00:03:03.795 --> 00:03:07.435
and just the yesterday or the night
before we were at a restaurant and

00:03:07.435 --> 00:03:10.620
low and behold the family sitting
beside us are recent immigrants.

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:12.780
I don't know how recent, but from Iran.

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:17.340
The Iranians respond so positively
when you speak to them in their

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:19.020
language, it's it's astounding.

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:23.550
And they also immediately ask you,
are you familiar with Persian poetry?

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:26.850
And of course, because Persian poetry
is very important to the Iranians.

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:28.530
Uh, but it's too difficult for me.

00:03:28.535 --> 00:03:30.420
So I, I disappoint them when I say no.

00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:35.970
Uh, but very often too, they'll ask
me if I speak Turkish because one in

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:42.390
five or 18 or whatever percent, 15%
of Iranians are of Azeri Turk origin.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:46.620
So therein, we begin to see that there's
this historical connection between

00:03:46.625 --> 00:03:52.350
Turks and Persians or Iranians now.

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:56.220
But the, the interesting thing, what I
found so fascinating is, is, is in this

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:03.330
series of, of episodes about Iranian
identity if we go back 2,500 years, To

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:09.060
the emperor, the, the empire of, of, uh,
Cyrus and, and I think the greatest extent

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:11.730
of the Persian empire was under Darius.

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:16.230
The great, I don't remember becasue
I forget it all, but the, the

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:21.510
old, uh, Achaemenid or however
it's pronounced Achaemenid empire

00:04:22.125 --> 00:04:24.735
was two thirds non Iranians.

00:04:25.305 --> 00:04:29.865
Uh, if we look at a map, it included,
you know, at, at various times, Greece

00:04:29.865 --> 00:04:34.455
and, and maybe the Northern part of
India, and you had Turkic people there.

00:04:34.455 --> 00:04:41.835
And of course, uh, originally you had the
Elamites and the Acadians because ancient

00:04:41.835 --> 00:04:44.805
Persia included uh, what is today Iraq.

00:04:45.224 --> 00:04:51.405
It included, you know, the fertile
Crescent from which the Abrahamic

00:04:51.405 --> 00:04:57.795
religions came eventually Islam, but
initially, uh, Judaism and Christianity.

00:04:58.784 --> 00:05:05.745
So right off the bat, it was a
multiethnic, multilingual empire.

00:05:06.045 --> 00:05:08.165
And so what was the
identity at that point?

00:05:08.615 --> 00:05:12.480
Of course there, people guess at it,
but apparently Herodotus the Greek,

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:19.740
uh, historian said that people who were
identified as Persian, Parse or whatever

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:25.230
term was used, they had certain advantages
over non Persians within that empire.

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:29.310
So obviously there was a sense
of Persian identity at that time.

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:36.120
Uh, when Alexander the Great of
Macedonia invaded he tried to bring

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:41.760
in Greeks and make Iran more Greek,
which didn't really please the locals

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:44.790
who've preferred their Persian identity.

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:47.040
So there was some kind
of Persian identity.

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:51.990
The Persians also had their own
religion, the Zoroastrian religion,

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:57.210
which was essentially the, the state
religion as near as, as we can tell.

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:04.230
So there's this identity as part of
the, uh, Achaemenid empire, uh, which

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:09.210
included many ethnic groups, uh,
Cyrus in his great constitution was,

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:14.010
uh, very say magnanimous, but very
tolerant of different religions and

00:06:14.015 --> 00:06:15.510
different, different ethnic groups.

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:19.590
And maybe he had no choice because
they were, they outnumbered

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:21.390
the Persians in his empire.

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:23.970
Um, so there was that.

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:32.490
And then after, um, Alexander, the uh, the
Greeks under, I, I think I might have said

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:37.260
it or it wasn't Alexander who tried to
bring in Greeks and make Iran more Greek.

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:39.420
It was Seleucus who was...

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:45.615
seleucus or however it's pronounced
who basically, as the, uh, the

00:06:45.615 --> 00:06:49.740
Alexander's empire was split he
ended up with iran basically.

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:52.500
And he tried to make it more
Greek, which didn't work.

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:57.420
But after a few hundred years, there
was once again, an Iranian dynasty,

00:06:57.420 --> 00:06:59.790
an Iranian control of their country.

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:05.490
And, uh, so there is now this, obviously
there was a sense of identity as Iranians

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:11.640
through the language, through their
Zoroastrian , uh, religion, uh, through a

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:15.990
sense of where they lived through identity
with identifying with their ruler.

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:17.995
I don't know, they even had a flag.

00:07:17.995 --> 00:07:21.420
They had certain things that
represented identity for them.

00:07:22.230 --> 00:07:28.980
Uh, and then passing through my
forgetfulness, uh, you know, you

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:33.450
had the, uh, what we call the, the
Partheon empire, I think, which,

00:07:33.455 --> 00:07:36.750
uh, It goes under a different name
in Persian, which I've forgotten.

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:41.040
And then the Sasanian period where
Iran was fighting with Rome all

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:45.930
the time and eventually the Arab
invasion and Islam was introduced.

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:47.280
So now we have Islam.

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:52.020
So there's identity through language,
identity through territory, identity

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:58.860
through this Persian ethnicity, which for
a long time was a minority, uh, you know,

00:07:59.190 --> 00:08:06.164
group within the greater Persian empire,
and now we have identity through religion

00:08:06.734 --> 00:08:10.515
and the religion was mostly Sunni.

00:08:11.655 --> 00:08:16.515
And, uh, and then around the 10th,
11th century, we have a series

00:08:16.515 --> 00:08:20.594
of Turkish dynasties in Iran.

00:08:21.164 --> 00:08:25.844
And so the, again, I'm going from
memory, but as I read it, I enjoy it.

00:08:25.844 --> 00:08:28.875
And then I forget it, but so Arab was...

00:08:28.875 --> 00:08:32.565
the Arabic language was perhaps more
prestigious through religion, but

00:08:32.565 --> 00:08:38.414
eventually the, basically the education
standards in, in Iran were higher

00:08:38.419 --> 00:08:40.365
than amongst the Arab conquerors.

00:08:40.370 --> 00:08:45.855
And so, um, Persian had a, um, pride of
place in the administration and so forth,

00:08:46.095 --> 00:08:50.685
but then the Turks came in and they
tended to speak Turkish in the court.

00:08:51.045 --> 00:08:53.895
And then at a certain point,
perhaps coinciding with the Mongol

00:08:53.895 --> 00:09:00.915
invasion, there was a revival of,
uh, the Farsi or Persian language.

00:09:00.945 --> 00:09:06.225
So there was, again, you, you can sense
through all of that as this person quotes

00:09:06.225 --> 00:09:10.395
the different sources, debating about,
you know, what represented the Iranian

00:09:10.395 --> 00:09:16.185
identity and so forth, uh, that, uh, there
was a revival of the Persian language.

00:09:17.145 --> 00:09:23.955
And then with the Safavid dynasty,
Um, the shiite religion is

00:09:23.955 --> 00:09:25.875
established as the national religion.

00:09:26.145 --> 00:09:27.945
And so now we have an identity.

00:09:28.455 --> 00:09:30.675
So we have a Islamic identity.

00:09:30.675 --> 00:09:35.415
We have a Shia identity as opposed
to the Sunni Ottoman empire.

00:09:35.895 --> 00:09:38.955
Uh, we have of course
that Iranian identity.

00:09:39.045 --> 00:09:42.855
So we have this accumulation of different
identities and getting back to the

00:09:42.855 --> 00:09:45.285
poetry, uh, which I always get asked.

00:09:46.065 --> 00:09:50.685
Uh, the sort of, one of the main
periods of flowering of poetry in Iran

00:09:50.685 --> 00:09:54.255
was during the time that the Mongols,
I think initially the Turks, this

00:09:54.615 --> 00:09:59.865
again, I'm going from memory and then
the Mongols, the Ilkhan dynasties.

00:09:59.865 --> 00:10:05.955
And so there was this flowering of poetry,
which focused to a large degree on, you

00:10:05.955 --> 00:10:12.315
know, myths and traditions and, and, and
the history of the great flowering of

00:10:12.645 --> 00:10:15.405
the Persian empire back 2,500 years ago.

00:10:15.705 --> 00:10:20.415
So the Iranians have been imbued with
this sense of their identity, which

00:10:20.415 --> 00:10:23.025
consists of all these different elements.

00:10:23.355 --> 00:10:27.315
And depending on which Iranian you
speak to, he may identify, or she may

00:10:27.315 --> 00:10:31.785
identify more with the period prior
the greatness of the, you know, Persian

00:10:31.785 --> 00:10:37.965
empire or more may identify more with,
um, you know, Islam and so forth.

00:10:37.970 --> 00:10:39.615
So there's there's as an in...

00:10:39.615 --> 00:10:44.685
and even today, as I mentioned earlier,
there's this ethnic mixture in Iran.

00:10:44.685 --> 00:10:49.785
You have Azeri Turks, you have Turkmans
who are small number, but were very

00:10:49.785 --> 00:10:55.815
influential initially, uh, in, um, some
of the dynasties that were established.

00:10:55.965 --> 00:10:58.074
You have Kurds, you have Arabs.

00:10:58.574 --> 00:11:01.545
This, and then you have various,
uh, sort of nomad groups.

00:11:01.545 --> 00:11:04.035
So there's quite a variety
of peoples within Iran.

00:11:04.155 --> 00:11:10.365
And so probably today, the identity is
one of Iran as a nation, the country

00:11:10.365 --> 00:11:17.204
of Iran with all of the ethnic, uh, uh,
you know, minorities that they have.

00:11:18.135 --> 00:11:23.535
And in this, um, series, it has pointed
out that amongst the people in the

00:11:23.535 --> 00:11:27.645
Middle East, the Iranians have a much
stronger sense of national identity.

00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:30.510
You know, are you proud
of being an Iranian?

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:34.380
And so a, a much higher percentage
in Iran will say, yes, I am.

00:11:34.680 --> 00:11:39.090
Whereas in places like Jordan or Egypt,
apparently the identification is more

00:11:39.090 --> 00:11:41.910
with Islam and less with the country.

00:11:42.150 --> 00:11:44.130
So all of this is interesting.

00:11:44.730 --> 00:11:48.750
The fact that I'm able to read and
listen to all of this information

00:11:48.750 --> 00:11:53.610
and kind of span the period
from 2,500 years ago to today.

00:11:54.045 --> 00:11:59.975
Uh, try and picture Iraq when it was part
of Iran and or Persia and now and stuff.

00:12:00.465 --> 00:12:05.084
Is, uh, to a large extent due to
Sahra and her ability to create

00:12:05.089 --> 00:12:07.365
material for me and find stuff for me.

00:12:07.725 --> 00:12:14.805
I wish I could find someone to do the same
for Arabic, uh, Arabic as a whole, or,

00:12:15.015 --> 00:12:21.450
you know, Egypt or, or, uh, north Africa
or the, the Levant, you know, Syria,

00:12:21.450 --> 00:12:23.610
Lebanon, uh, Palestine, and so forth.

00:12:24.030 --> 00:12:29.370
So, because that to me is the fun of
language learning is, is exploring through

00:12:29.370 --> 00:12:33.000
language, history, culture, people.

00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:37.320
And, uh, so I'm very grateful to Sahra
and I enjoy doing this in Persian

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:42.330
and I will certainly be looking
forward to doing it in Arabic and in

00:12:42.330 --> 00:12:44.460
other languages, uh, moving forward.

00:12:45.090 --> 00:12:47.055
So there you have it.

00:12:47.115 --> 00:12:50.055
Uh, I will leave you with a couple of
videos on this issue of language and

00:12:50.055 --> 00:12:54.255
culture, and I'd be interested in your
own reactions and how you deal with,

00:12:54.615 --> 00:12:58.064
uh, and to what extent you're attracted
to the idea of, of learning about

00:12:58.064 --> 00:12:59.895
culture and history through language.

00:12:59.895 --> 00:13:00.824
Thanks for listening.

00:13:00.824 --> 00:13:01.214
Bye for now.

